[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Scotch Mieses for Black"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LuXFyksyKBI"]
[Date "2025.09.10"]
[Round "1"]
[White "Scotch Mieses"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Ben Finegold video"]
[Orientation "black"]
[Opening "Scotch Game: Mieses Variation"]
[SourceTitle "Playing For a Win as Black in the Scotch Mieses, with GM Ben Finegold"]
[SourceURL "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LuXFyksyKBI"]

1. e4 e5
2. Nf3 Nc6
3. d4 exd4
4. Nxd4 Nf6
{Finegold frames `4...Nf6` as the fighting Scotch choice for this lecture; White either transposes with Nc3 or enters the Mieses by taking on c6 and playing e5.}
5. Nxc6 bxc6
6. e5 Qe7
{Do not hurry to retreat the knight. First pin the e-pawn to the queen and make White spend a move unpinning it.}
7. Qe2 Nd5
{The knight goes to d5 only after `Qe2` has clarified White's setup. It blocks the pawn, occupies a central square, and prepares the sharp `c4` fight.}
8. c4
{Finegold calls this the explosive old main try. White gains space and attacks the knight, but the c-pawn and queen can become tactical targets.}
8... Nb6
{This is the exact weak-deck move. The knight attacks c4, so White must decide whether to defend the pawn, chase the knight, or accept that Black will win it later.}
(8... Ba6
{Finegold's main playing-for-a-win idea is also important: pin the c4-pawn to the queen, so cxd5 is not really available.}
9. b3 O-O-O
10. Bb2 Qg5
{Once the bishop is on b2, the queen can come to g5 and make White's kingside development awkward.}
11. Qf3 Bb4+
12. Kd1 Rhe8
{Black's plan is active-piece play: long castle, rook to e8, queen checks, and pressure on e5/c4 rather than quiet equality.})
9. Nd2
{A normal way to defend c4 and prepare development.}
9... Ba6
{The pin motif still matters even after `...Nb6`: Black wants the c-pawn tied to the queen and the light-square bishop outside the pawn chain.}
10. b3 O-O-O
11. Bb2 Re8
12. g3
{White often needs time to finish development; Finegold emphasizes that Black should use that time to open lines around the uncastled king.}
12... d5
{The thematic central break. When White has invested moves in holding c4/e5, Black opens the center for the rooks and bishops.}
13. exd6 Qxd6
{Black has active pieces, pressure on the dark squares, and clear play against White's king and queenside pawns. Remember the three Mieses handles: pin c4 with `...Ba6`, attack it with `...Nb6`, and open the center with `...d5`.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Scotch Gambit for Black"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9uomdqN9ihw"]
[Date "2022.11.03"]
[Round "2"]
[White "Scotch Gambit Ng5"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Miodrag Perunovic video"]
[Orientation "black"]
[Opening "Scotch Game: Scotch Gambit"]
[SourceTitle "Getting Aggressive vs Fearsome Scotch Gambit!!"]
[SourceURL "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9uomdqN9ihw"]

1. e4 e5
2. Nf3 Nc6
3. d4 exd4
4. Bc4 Nf6
{Perunovic's Black-side Scotch Gambit setup. The bishop on c4 and knight jump to g5 aim straight at f7, so Black must know the concrete antidote.}
5. Ng5 d5
{The key resource. Perunovic stresses that this move opposes the f7 attack while hitting the bishop and making White clarify the center.}
6. exd5 Na5
{This is the direct weak-deck move: chase the bishop away from c4 so f7 is no longer under a double attack.}
(6... Qe7+
{Perunovic's precision move: check first, then chase the bishop. It avoids the bad habit of recapturing on d5 too early.}
7. Kf1 Na5
8. Bb5+ c6
9. dxc6 bxc6
10. Be2 h6
11. Nf3 Qc5
{Black has displaced White's king, chased the attacking knight, and can recover or attack the d5/c-file material later.})
(6... Nxd5?
{The move Perunovic warns against. Recapturing too soon lets White's f7 tactics and queen checks become dangerous.}
7. Nxf7 Kxf7
8. Qh5+
{Black has walked into the kind of attack the whole `...d5/...Na5` concept is meant to avoid.})
7. Bb5+
{White's main check after `...Na5`.}
7... c6
8. dxc6 bxc6
{Black accepts the structural cost because the open game and bishop pair give practical compensation.}
9. Be2 h6
{Kick the knight. Once the knight leaves g5, the immediate attack on f7 has disappeared.}
10. Nf3 Bd6
11. O-O O-O
12. Nbd2 c5
{Black's plan is simple enough to remember: play `...d5`, chase the bishop with `...Na5`, meet the check with `...c6`, recapture with the b-pawn, kick the knight with `...h6`, and finish development. The remaining d4-pawn and active bishops give Black the initiative.} *
